ate-type="received"><day>15</day><month>2</month><year>2001</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>16</day><month>5</month><year>2001</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>16</day><month>5</month><year>2001</year></date></history><pub-history><event event-type="pmc-release"><date><day>02</day><month>07</month><year>2002</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-live"><date><day>19</day><month>09</month><year>2002</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-last-change"><date iso-8601-date="2026-09-07 22:06:25.163"><day>07</day><month>09</month><year>2026</year></date></event></pub-history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright © 2001 European Molecular Biology Organization</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2001</copyright-year></permissions><self-uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="pmc-pdf" xlink:href="cde346.pdf"/><abstract><p>The three-dimensional structure of a bacterial superantigen, <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic> enterotoxin H (SEH), bound to human major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class II (HLA-DR1) has been determined by X-ray crystallography to 2.6 Å resolution (1HXY). The superantigen binds on top of HLA-DR1 in a completely different way from earlier co-crystallized superantigens from <italic toggle="yes">S.aureus</italic>. SEH interacts with high affinity through a zinc ion with the β1 chain of HLA-DR1 and also with the peptide presented by HLA-DR1. The structure suggests that all superantigens interacting with MHC class II in a zinc-dependent manner present the superantigen in a common way. This suggests a new model for ternary complex formation with the T-cell receptor (TCR), in which a contact between the TCR and the MHC class II is unlikely.</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>MHC class II/staphylococcal enterotoxin/superantigen/X-ray crystallography/zinc</kwd></kwd-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-qastatus</meta-name><meta-value>0</meta-value></custom-meta><